      "assistant_text": "Perfect. Now I can answer your question directly:\n\n## The Connection\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are intimately related\u2014in fact, they form a unified architectural pattern. I\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]].\n\nHere\u0027s the core insight from [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]]:\n\n**Credential scoping is only *reliably enforceable* because of the single composition point.** The credential-scoping model protects the Hub by injecting the Anthropic API key only into child-process environments\u2014never into the Hub\u0027s own memory. But that security guarantee only holds if there\u0027s a single, controlled place where secrets are loaded.\n\nThat place is the same path resolver that handles all other runtime configuration. Because every runtime location\u2014the wiki root, the database, the secrets file, agent instruction directories\u2014is resolved in exactly one spot at startup, adding credential injection there is natural and practical. It becomes the only place where secrets are touched, which makes the scoping model enforceable rather than aspirational.\n\nWithout the single composition point, you\u0027d risk different parts of the codebase accidentally discovering the secret elsewhere. With it, credential scoping becomes structural: secrets are simply another kind of runtime location, validated once and injected precisely into the right child processes.",
